511.3 CART SEMANTIC PROOF OBLIGATIONS
=====================================
Domain-papers/500–599 Science
511 General principles of mathematics
511.3 Coordinate‑Free Algebra Relational Tensioner (CART)

CART = (C, K, D, C/K, H)

This document defines the **semantic proof obligations** required to ensure
that any implementation, formalization, or interpretation of CART satisfies
its intended meaning. These obligations guarantee that the semantics of
resolution, defect, identity, and hierarchy remain stable, invariant, and
coordinate‑free.


============================================================
0. PURPOSE OF PROOF OBLIGATIONS
============================================================
Proof obligations ensure:

- semantic correctness  
- operator consistency  
- identity stability  
- ultrametric validity  
- relational‑mode coherence  
- tier‑depth soundness  

They are required for any formal semantics, computational model,
or mathematical instantiation of CART.


============================================================
1. CARRIER SEMANTIC OBLIGATIONS (C)
============================================================

Obligation C‑1 — Non‑Geometric Domain
    Prove: No element of C contains coordinates, metrics, or embeddings.

Obligation C‑2 — Structural Closure
    Prove: All native relational operations preserve membership in C.

Obligation C‑3 — Pre‑Resolution Purity
    Prove: No canonical identity is derivable without applying K.

Obligation C‑4 — Relational Consistency
    Prove: Every configuration in C is interpretable as a relational state.


============================================================
2. KERNEL SEMANTIC OBLIGATIONS (K)
============================================================

Obligation K‑1 — Idempotence
    Prove: For all x ∈ C, K(K(x)) = K(x).

Obligation K‑2 — Contractivity
    Prove: D(K(x)) ≤ D(x).

Obligation K‑3 — Canonicality
    Prove: K(x) is the unique canonical representative of [x].

Obligation K‑4 — Stability
    Prove: K(x) = x ⇒ x is semantically resolved.

Obligation K‑5 — Resolution Soundness
    Prove: K removes relational inconsistency without introducing new structure.


============================================================
3. DEFECT SEMANTIC OBLIGATIONS (D)
============================================================

Obligation D‑1 — Defect Definition
    Prove: D(x) = δ(x, K(x)) for a well‑defined mismatch function δ.

Obligation D‑2 — Zero‑Defect Identity
    Prove: D(x) = 0 ⇔ x = K(x).

Obligation D‑3 — Monotonicity Under Resolution
    Prove: D(K(x)) ≤ D(x).

Obligation D‑4 — Non‑Negativity
    Prove: D(x) ≥ 0 for all x ∈ C.

Obligation D‑5 — Semantic Invariance
    Prove: D depends only on relational mismatch, not coordinates or geometry.


============================================================
4. QUOTIENT SEMANTIC OBLIGATIONS (C/K)
============================================================

Obligation Q‑1 — Equivalence Relation
    Prove: x ~ y ⇔ K(x) = K(y) is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.

Obligation Q‑2 — Identity Class Correctness
    Prove: [x] = { y | K(y) = K(x) }.

Obligation Q‑3 — Canonical Representative
    Prove: K(x) ∈ [x] and is unique.

Obligation Q‑4 — Quotient Idempotence
    Prove: Q(Q(x)) = Q(x).

Obligation Q‑5 — Admissibility Soundness
    Prove: D(x) ≤ ε ⇒ [x] is admissible.

Obligation Q‑6 — Defect Independence
    Prove: Identity depends only on K, not on D.


============================================================
5. HIERARCHY SEMANTIC OBLIGATIONS (H)
============================================================

Obligation H‑1 — Ultrametric Validity
    Prove: u satisfies u(A,C) ≤ max(u(A,B), u(B,C)).

Obligation H‑2 — Zero‑Distance Identity
    Prove: K(x) = K(y) ⇒ u([x],[y]) = 0.

Obligation H‑3 — Tier Assignment Correctness
    Prove: H([x]) ∈ {Tier1, Tier2, Tier3}.

Obligation H‑4 — Depth Monotonicity
    Prove: u([a],[b]) < u([a],[c]) ⇒ H([b]) ≤ H([c]).

Obligation H‑5 — Hierarchy Idempotence
    Prove: H(H([x])) = H([x]).

Obligation H‑6 — Entanglement Semantics
    Prove: Tier depth corresponds to relational entanglement, not geometry.


============================================================
6. PIPELINE SEMANTIC OBLIGATIONS
============================================================

Obligation P‑1 — Pipeline Correctness
    Prove: x → K → D → Q → H is semantically well‑typed.

Obligation P‑2 — Pipeline Stability
    Prove: H(Q(D(K(x)))) = H(Q(K(x))).

Obligation P‑3 — Pipeline Idempotence
    Prove: Reapplying the pipeline yields identical results.

Obligation P‑4 — Mode Alignment
    Prove: R0→R1→R2→R3→R4 corresponds exactly to C→K→D→C/K→H.

Obligation P‑5 — Tier Gate Correctness
    Prove: Only R3 and R4 admit tier depth.


============================================================
7. RELATIONAL-MODE SEMANTIC OBLIGATIONS
============================================================

Obligation RM‑1 — Mode Soundness
    Prove: Each mode ⟦R_i⟧ corresponds to its component’s semantics.

Obligation RM‑2 — Mode Purity
    Prove: R0–R2 contain no tier semantics.

Obligation RM‑3 — Identity Mode Correctness
    Prove: R3 = ⟦C/K⟧.

Obligation RM‑4 — Hierarchy Mode Correctness
    Prove: R4 = ⟦H⟧ ∘ ⟦C/K⟧.

Obligation RM‑5 — Mode Transition Validity
    Prove: R0→R1→R2→R3→R4 is monotonic in semantic refinement.


============================================================
8. GLOBAL SEMANTIC CONSISTENCY OBLIGATIONS
============================================================

Obligation G‑1 — Coordinate‑Free Guarantee
    Prove: No semantic interpretation introduces coordinates.

Obligation G‑2 — Operator Coherence
    Prove: All operators commute with semantic interpretation.

Obligation G‑3 — Identity Preservation
    Prove: If K(x)=K(y), all semantic interpretations treat x and y identically.

Obligation G‑4 — Depth Preservation
    Prove: H(Q(x)) = H(Q(K(x))).

Obligation G‑5 — Semantic Completeness
    Prove: Every configuration has a well‑defined semantic pipeline result.


============================================================
9. ONE-LINE SUMMARY
============================================================
CART semantic proof obligations ensure that resolution, defect, identity,
and hierarchy behave exactly as intended in every formal, computational,
or mathematical interpretation of the coordinate‑free relational substrate.
